    "The raw Ed25519 publication/store APIs remain authenticated **under the exact caller-supplied public key**. Milestone 41A adds an optional explicit trust-policy layer rather than changing those compatibility APIs. `SnapshotTrustPolicy` bounds the policy to at most 64 Ed25519 public keys, gives every key a domain-separated SHA-256 key ID plus `Active`/`Revoked` state, and hashes the non-zero generation plus the complete sorted key/state set into a deterministic policy identity. `rotate()` requires a strictly later generation, preserves revoked keys as revoked, rejects unknown revocations, and cannot reactivate a revoked key through that transition.\n\n`store_snapshot_archive_trusted_ed25519_durable` and `materialize_snapshot_store_object_trusted_ed25519_atomic` resolve the declared signer through that policy **before store access**, then reuse the existing strict Ed25519 verification and durable/atomic store paths. Successful reports carry the exact policy generation/hash and signer key ID. Executable evidence proves an old key can authorize publication under generation 1, the same cryptographically valid old signature is rejected as `RevokedSigner` under generation 2 before a deliberately missing store root is touched, a new generation-2 key can deduplicate and materialize the same authenticated object, unknown signers fail at the same trust gate, and active membership never bypasses signature verification.\n\nThis is explicit trust-snapshot provenance and rotation/revocation semantics for the policy object the caller supplies. It is **not** a persistent trust store, certificate chain, key custody system, timestamp/validity service, secure policy distribution mechanism, hardware root of trust, compromise-recovery protocol, or anti-rollback guarantee. A caller that deliberately supplies an older policy snapshot can still obtain that older snapshot's decisions.\n",
